    <h1>VPN for Remote Work Abroad</h1>
    <p class="article-intro">Working remotely from Portugal, Spain, Morocco or anywhere outside France creates specific challenges: restricted connections, geo-blocked company tools, unreliable local networks. A VPN solves some of these problems — as long as it doesn't create new ones.</p>

    <h2>Real Remote Work Problems From Abroad</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Geo-restricted company tools</strong> — some corporate VPNs, ERPs or SaaS tools limit access to French IPs</li>
      <li><strong>Banking services</strong> — your bank may block or complicate access from a foreign IP</li>
      <li><strong>Unsecured local Wi-Fi</strong> — coworking spaces, cafés, Airbnbs: you don't control the network infrastructure</li>
      <li><strong>High latency</strong> — some countries have slow international connections to Europe</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a VPN Gives Remote Workers</h2>
    <p>A VPN with a France server gives you a French IP, solving geo-restriction issues. It also encrypts your traffic on local networks — essential when working from a coworking space or café whose network configuration you don't know.</p>

    <h2>Speed: The Make-or-Break Factor</h2>
    <p>This is where many VPNs fail for remote work. A video call, screen share, or online ERP access — all require a stable, fast connection. If your VPN cuts your bandwidth in half or by two thirds, remote work becomes painful.</p>
    <p>VulcainVPN is designed to avoid this. The server is <strong>dedicated and not overloaded</strong> — subscriber count is deliberately limited so each user has sufficient resources. If your local connection is good, your VPN will be fast.</p>

    <div class="article-highlight">
      <h3>In Practice for Remote Work</h3>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Video calls (Teams, Meet, Zoom)</strong> — preserved speed avoids drops and pixelation</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>ERP / company tool access</strong> — French IP, access as if you were in France</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>Coworking / café</strong> — encrypted traffic on a network you don't control</p>
      <p><span class="checkmark">➤</span> <strong>3 simultaneous devices</strong> — work laptop, work phone, tablet all covered</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Corporate VPN vs Personal VPN</h2>
    <p>Your company may have its own VPN for internal resources. VulcainVPN is complementary, not competing: you can use both. The corporate VPN connects you to internal resources; VulcainVPN secures the rest of your traffic (personal browsing, banking, private communications).</p>

    <h2>Protocols for Difficult Connections</h2>
    <p>From some countries, international connections to Europe are unstable or slow. VulcainVPN offers three protocols:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>WireGuard</strong> — optimal on stable, fast connections</li>
      <li><strong>Hysteria2</strong> — specifically designed for high-latency or unstable connections, maintains decent speeds even on difficult networks</li>
      <li><strong>Shadowsocks</strong> — useful on networks that filter standard VPN connections</li>
    </ul>

